{0 Web Crypto}

{!Workero.Crypto} wraps [crypto.subtle], the Web Crypto API of the Workers
runtime. Data goes in and out as OCaml strings that hold raw bytes. The
functions wait for the runtime, so call them inside a handler. For random
bytes and UUIDs, see {{!page-globals}Timers, time, and randomness}.

{1 Digests, HMAC and passwords}

{!Workero.Crypto.digest} hashes a string with SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384 or
SHA-512, chosen by {!Workero.Crypto.hash}. {!Workero.Crypto.Digest_stream} hashes
a stream that you write chunk by chunk, without keeping the data.
{!Workero.Crypto.pbkdf2} and {!Workero.Crypto.hkdf} derive bytes. Deployed
Workers allow at most 100000 PBKDF2 iterations.

{!Workero.Crypto.timing_safe_equal} compares two strings in time that does not
depend on where they differ. Use it for MACs and tokens, not [=]. It is a
Cloudflare extension of [crypto.subtle]. Strings of different lengths are not
equal, and the call says so at once.

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let sign_and_check secret body =
  let key = Crypto.Key.import_hmac Crypto.Sha256 secret in
  let signature = Crypto.hmac_sign key body in
  Crypto.hmac_verify key ~signature body

let handler _req _env _ctx =
  Response.text (string_of_bool (sign_and_check "secret" "body"))

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

[sign_and_check] gives [true] for any non-empty [secret].

{1 Keys}

A {!Workero.Crypto.Key.t} is a [CryptoKey]. Its type names the algorithm. For an
asymmetric key it also names the half, {!Workero.Crypto.Key.type-public} or
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.type-private_}. The compiler rejects an HMAC key where an RSA
key goes, and a public key where a signing key goes.

There are three ways to get a key:

- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-generate} makes a symmetric key, and
  {!Workero.Crypto.Key.generate_pair} makes a pair, from an algorithm such as
  [Hmac Sha256], [Aes_gcm], [Rsa_pss Sha256], [Ecdsa P256] or [Ed25519].
- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-import} reads a key. The constructor of its first
  argument gives the format and the algorithm: [Secret_raw], [Secret_jwk],
  [Public_raw], [Public_spki], [Public_jwk], [Private_pkcs8] or [Private_jwk].
  The type of the result follows. [raw], [spki] and [pkcs8] data are bytes
  (DER for the last two). [jwk] data is the JSON text of a JSON Web Key.
- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_hmac} and {!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_aes_gcm} are
  the short forms for raw keys.

Keys are not extractable unless you pass [~extractable:true]. The public key of
a generated pair is always extractable. {!Workero.Crypto.Key.export_raw},
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.export_jwk}, {!Workero.Crypto.Key.export_spki} and
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.export_pkcs8} read a key back. They raise
{!Workero.Js_error} for a key that is not extractable or a format that the
algorithm does not have, such as [raw] for an RSA key. The usages of a key
follow its algorithm: you do not choose them.
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.algorithm} and {!Workero.Crypto.Key.usages} read the
[algorithm] and [usages] properties of a key. The first gives JSON text, such as
[{"name":"AES-GCM","length":256}].

An RSA pair has the public exponent 65537. Pass [~public_exponent:3] to
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.generate_pair} for 3. [workerd] rejects other exponents.

This handler makes an Ed25519 pair, signs, and verifies with the exported
public key:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let pair = Crypto.Key.(generate_pair ~extractable:true Ed25519) in
  let signature = Crypto.ed25519_sign pair.private_ "hello" in
  let spki = Crypto.Key.export_spki pair.public in
  let public = Crypto.Key.(import (Public_spki Ed25519) spki) in
  Response.text (string_of_bool (Crypto.ed25519_verify public ~signature "hello"))

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{1 Signatures}

- {!Workero.Crypto.rsassa_pkcs1_sign} and {!Workero.Crypto.rsassa_pkcs1_verify}:
  RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5. The hash belongs to the key.
- {!Workero.Crypto.rsa_pss_sign} and {!Workero.Crypto.rsa_pss_verify}: RSA-PSS
  with a salt length in bytes.
- {!Workero.Crypto.ecdsa_sign} and {!Workero.Crypto.ecdsa_verify}: ECDSA on
  P-256, P-384 or P-521, with the hash chosen at each call. A signature is [r]
  followed by [s], not DER.
- {!Workero.Crypto.ed25519_sign} and {!Workero.Crypto.ed25519_verify}.

A verify function gives [false] for a wrong or malformed signature.

{1 Encryption}

- {!Workero.Crypto.aes_gcm_encrypt} and {!Workero.Crypto.aes_gcm_decrypt}. The
  cipher text ends with the 16-byte tag. Decryption gives [None] when
  authentication fails. Never use one [iv] twice with one key.
- {!Workero.Crypto.aes_cbc_encrypt} and {!Workero.Crypto.aes_cbc_decrypt}, and
  {!Workero.Crypto.aes_ctr_encrypt} and {!Workero.Crypto.aes_ctr_decrypt}. These
  modes do not authenticate the data. Prefer AES-GCM.
- {!Workero.Crypto.rsa_oaep_encrypt} and {!Workero.Crypto.rsa_oaep_decrypt}, with
  an optional label. The message must be short: a key of [n] bits and a hash of
  [h] bytes take [n / 8 - 2h - 2] bytes. Decryption gives [None] for a wrong key
  or label.

{1 Key agreement}

{!Workero.Crypto.ecdh_derive_bits} and {!Workero.Crypto.x25519_derive_bits} give
the shared secret of your private key and the public key of the other party.
Both parties get the same bytes. Pass the secret through
{!Workero.Crypto.hkdf} before you use it as a key.

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let shared_key () =
  let a = Crypto.Key.(generate_pair (Ecdh Crypto.P256)) in
  let b = Crypto.Key.(generate_pair (Ecdh Crypto.P256)) in
  let secret = Crypto.ecdh_derive_bits a.private_ ~public:b.public ~length:32 in
  Crypto.hkdf Crypto.Sha256 ~key:secret ~salt:"" ~info:"app" ~length:32
]}

{1 Deriving keys}

{!Workero.Crypto.pbkdf2_key}, {!Workero.Crypto.hkdf_key},
{!Workero.Crypto.ecdh_derive_key} and {!Workero.Crypto.x25519_derive_key} are
[deriveKey]: they make a key for an algorithm, such as [Aes_gcm] or
[Hmac Sha256], straight from a password, a key or a shared secret. The result
is not extractable unless you pass [~extractable:true]. [~bits] sets the key
length, as in {!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-generate}.

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let key_of_password password salt =
  Crypto.pbkdf2_key Crypto.Sha256 ~password ~salt ~iterations:100_000
    Crypto.Key.Aes_gcm
]}

{1 Wrapping keys}

{!Workero.Crypto.wrap_key} exports a key in a format and encrypts it with an
AES-KW, AES-GCM, AES-CBC, AES-CTR or RSA-OAEP key. AES-CBC and AES-CTR do not
authenticate the wrapped key: prefer AES-KW or AES-GCM. {!Workero.Crypto.unwrap_key} decrypts and
imports it, with the same [import] values as {!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-import}. Wrap
with the public RSA-OAEP key and unwrap with the private one. A key from
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_aes_gcm} cannot wrap. Use
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-generate} or {!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-import}.

{1 Errors}

Bad arguments that the library can check raise [Invalid_argument]. A rejection
from the runtime raises {!Workero.Js_error}: a key in the wrong format, a
non-extractable key that you export, or a failed unwrap.
{!Workero.Crypto.Key.val-import} of an unfit key also raises it.

Not available: Ed448 and X448 ([workerd] does not know them), and RSA public
exponents other than 3 and 65537 ([workerd] rejects them). See the {{:https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero/issues}open issues}.
